From 362b387e21558890bc6512e7dbfda88ffc3e7e0e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: i <v@g>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 18:27:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] assurance.watch: watch the suppliers, not just the machines
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The machine watch answers "did anything on my floor change". This answers the
other half, and it is the half that wakes somebody up: did the world change
underneath machines that did not.

A supplier publishes a signed advisory at 09:00. Nothing on the plant floor
moved, every manifest still matches, and the machine watch reports QUIET —
correctly. The integrator finds out when their insurer asks. That gap is the
difference between a tool and a subscription: something that tells you about a
problem you did not know to look for is worth paying for every month, and
something you have to remember to run is not.

WatchConfig gains `feeds`: a supplier id, a feed path and a public key path. The
key is a separate file deliberately. A feed that carries its own key
authenticates nothing, because the forged feed will carry one too; it has to be
a file the operator put there, obtained by a route the advisories do not travel
on.

Four judgements, and each of them is a way this is normally built wrong.

**New is not the same as outstanding.** An advisory reported last week does not
reappear as new, and is not forgotten either — it is carried as outstanding
until the fleet stops matching it or the supplier withdraws it. A watch that
re-reports everything trains the reader to skim by the third Monday, at which
point it has become an expensive way to generate silence.

**A feed that cannot be checked is DEGRADED, never quiet.** Missing file, wrong
key, broken chain, bad signature: all exit 2, and nothing in an unverified feed
is acted on. This is where a signed-advisory mechanism most easily becomes
decoration — the fetch fails, verification is skipped, the run reports quiet,
and the absence of alarms is read as the absence of danger.

**A withdrawal is a finding.** If an advisory that affected this fleet is
retracted, somebody here very likely changed a machine because of it, and
nothing else in this industry tells them. Announced exactly once, and only to a
fleet that was told about the original: an advisory published and retracted
between two runs is not mentioned, because nothing here was acted on.

**Stop-use is said first and alone.** An advisory the supplier marked stop_use
against a machine that matched is not one line among forty; it is the reason
this ran.

The advisory pass runs after the machine pass, against the manifests this run
has just sealed. Asking first would report yesterday's fleet against today's
advisories, which is the one combination guaranteed to be wrong.

checks_skipped gains the one that matters most operationally: this watch learns
of an advisory when the subscribed file changes, whatever puts the supplier's
feed there is outside this system, and a sync that silently stopped looks
exactly like a supplier with nothing to report.

Tests: 18 new, 719 total, all passing. ruff clean, mypy clean on both new
modules. Exercised live before committing across four runs of a real two-machine
fleet: an advisory arriving against still machines, the same advisory going
quiet on the second run, a withdrawal announced once with the consequence
spelled out, and a deleted feed file coming back DEGRADED rather than QUIET.

